A||N||3||grinthock||Tire Size - What can I use||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||10-22-2001||03:55 PM||||Trying to understand tire sizes here. I have a 87GT with the 15" rims, what can I put on them?  Size wise, is there some sort of rule, so wide VS so high is acceptable with a rim of X size... I also have a 2000 Dakota I need to get tires for and have the same question.  thx<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000001||MinnGreenGT||10-22-2001||04:09 PM||||Try this: <A HREF="http://www.miata.net/garage/tirecalc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.miata.net/garage/tirecalc.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||10-22-2001||08:02 PM||||The tire tricks are: The closer you keep the total outside diameter the less your speedometer will be off. The big thing is not to get too wide of a tire for the wheel size.<BR>Example: On a 15" wheel like your stock GT, You don't want to go larger than a 235. You can fit 245's but the tire will roll under more due to unsupported weight on the wider sidewall. This only affects handling slightly. Below is the recommended max tire size and the max you can put on safely. Exceeding the max safety could cause the tire to "blow-out" from the wheel during "aggressive" driving or hard cornering. The brand of tires and amount of air pressure they take can also affect how a wider tire handles on a thinner wheel.<BR>A general rule of thumb is:<BR>6" wide rec 225 max 235<BR>7" wide rec 235 max 245<BR>8" wide rec 245 max 265<P>The tire calculator that Minn posted will tell you the differences and at the bottom show how much it affects the speedometer.<BR><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||10-22-2001||11:19 PM||||Also check the tire manufacturer's specs.  They list a rim width range for each tire size and a recommended or a "measured rim width" that is the standard width they use to measure the tire dimensions.<P>The shorter your sidewall, the wider you need the wheel to be for a given width.  <BR>Example:<BR>a 235/60-15 may fit on a 6.5" wide rim, but a 235/40-15 would need a 7.5" wide rim (these aren't real specs, just an illustration).  You need a wider rim with the shorter sidewall since it can't give as much - the acceptable width range gets smaller.||||reg||1
